  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 630 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Potential for heavy rain continues across the \nsoutheast today. A Flood Watch remains in effect for south-central \nVA through this morning and Hampton Roads and northeast NC through \nthis evening. \n\n630am Update.. Latest analysis indicates the cold front has\npushed nearly to the NC/VA border. As a result, the heaviest\nrain is now limited to the NC counties and extreme southeast\nVirginia. As a result, the Flood Watch was cancelled for\neverywhere except those areas. Light rain will persist in the\nareas previously in the Flood Watch, but the threat of flooding\nhas largely diminished in the cancelled zones. \n\nPrevious Discussion..\n\nA cold front continues to slowly drop across the \nMid-Atlantic and was located across northern counties of the \nforecast area as of early morning analysis. A mid level trough \nis positioned over the east coast with the axis starting to \npivot into central VA. Latest radar shows showers with a few \nrumbles of thunder out ahead of the front filling in across \nsoutheast VA and interior NE NC. With PW values between 2-2.2\", \nthere's plenty of moisture to work with to bring heavy rain to \nthe area. The front will eventually drop south and bring dry air\nwith it. However, the snail's pace speed of the front likely \nmeans wet conditions in the south/southeast through at least \nthis evening. Despite drought conditions, multiple rounds of \nheavy rainfall may cause flooding. The Excessive Rainfall \nOutlook from the WPC highlights NE NC and southside Hampton \nRoads in a Moderate Risk (lvl 3/4), meaning the potential for \nnumerous instances of flooding. A Slight Risk (lvl 2/4)buffers \nthe Moderate and includes the rest of southeast VA. Rain may \nlinger across NE NC and extreme SE VA through tonight or even \nearly Friday depending on where exactly the front settles, but \nrain rates should be overall lower. A Flood Watch remains in \neffect for southern VA and NE NC. The watch is scheduled to \nexpire for most of the VA counties (excluding southside Hampton \nRoads) at 1pm today, though several counties along the northern \nand western edge of it will likely get trimmed before then as \nthe heavier rain shifts south. The QPF for today and tonight is \n2-3\" across NE NC and extreme SE VA on top of the 1-2\" that is \nexpected in SE VA before sunrise. \n\nFor the rest of the FA, today will be a teaser for early fall \nconditions with highs in the upper 70s and dewpoints dropping into \nthe low 60s and maybe even upper 50s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below average temperatures are expected tomorrow \nthrough the weekend, before trending back to seasonal for late July \nearly next week. There are daily chances for afternoon storms, but \noverall drier than the current wet pattern.\n\nThe cold front will eventually make it south of the area sometime \ntonight or early Friday, but there will be enough lingering \nmoisture for at least some chc to low-end likely PoPs in far SE \nVA and NE NC Fri and Fri night. The FA will then be positioned \nunder an UL trough over the weekend with onshore flow in place \nat the sfc. This will lead to cooler, below-average \ntemperatures for a few days. Highs on Fri and Sat will be in \nthe upper 70s to around 80. Warming up slightly for Sunday with \nhighs in the low to mid 80s. Near-average temps return early \nnext week. Looking to be overall a dry weekend, but a few \nisolated showers/storms cannot be ruled out during the \nafternoons.",
